:[{"Adt":[{"id":"Tuple","generics":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}},null,null]},[{"Move":{"kind":{"Local":4},"ty":{"Deduplicated":4418}}},{"Move":{"kind":{"Local":7},"ty":{"Deduplicated":4418}}}]]}]},"comments_before":[]},{"span":{"data":{"file_id":50,"beg":{"line":438,"col":31},"end":{"line":438,"col":32}},"generated_from_span":null},"id":16140,"kind":{"StorageDead":7},"comments_before":[]},{"span":{"data":{"file_id":50,"beg":{"line":438,"col":31},"end":{"line":438,"col":32}},"generated_from_span":null},"id":16141,"kind":{"StorageDead":4},"comments_before":[]},{"span":{"data":{"file_id":50,"beg":{"line":439,"col":5},"end":{"line":439,"col":5}},"generated_from_span":null},"id":16142,"kind":"Return","comments_before":[]}]},"comments":[]}}},{"def_id":464,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["spqr",0]},{"Ident":["encoding",0]},{"Ident":["gf",0]},{"Ident":["reduce",0]},{"Ident":["poly_reduce",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":50,"beg":{"line":490,"col":4},"end":{"line":499,"col":5}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":"pub const fn poly_reduce(v: u32) -> u16 {\n        let mut v = v;\n        let i1 = (v >> 24) as usize;\n        v ^= (REDUCE_BYTES[i1] as u32) << 8;\n        let shifted_v = (v >> 16) as usize;\n        let i2 = shifted_v & 0xFF;\n        hax_lib::fstar!(\"logand_lemma $shifted_v (mk_usize 255)\");\n        v ^= REDUCE_BYTES[i2] as u32;\n        v as u16\n    }","attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" This is a somewhat optimized reduction that's architecture-agnostic."},{"DocComment":" When reducing, we look from the highest- to lowest-order bits in the"},{"DocComment":" range 31..16, and we clear each 1 we find by XOR-ing with the POLY,"},{"DocComment":" whose topmost bit (1<<16) is set.  POLY is 17 bit long, meaning that"},{"DocComment":" we clear the topmost bit while affecting the 16 bits below it."},{"DocComment":" Now, let's consider the topmost BYTE of a u32.  Assuming that byte is"},{"DocComment":" some constant C, we will always perform the same set of (up to) 8"},{"DocComment":" XORs on the 2 bytes below C, and these can be precompted as a 16-byte"},{"DocComment":" XOR against those bytes.  Also, once we're done processing C, we never"},{"DocComment":" use its data again, as we (correctly) assume that it will be zero'd out."},{"DocComment":" So, given the (big-endian) u32 with bytes Cxyz, no matter what xyz are,"},{"DocComment":" we will always do:"},{"DocComment":"    Cxyz ^ Cab0 -> 0Dwz"},{"DocComment":" where the bytes `ab` are dependent entirely on C.  Assume again that"},{"DocComment":" we now have a new u32 with bytes 0Dwz.  We will again XOR this based"},{"DocComment":" entirely on the bits in D, and it will look something like:"},{"DocComment":"    0Dwz ^ 0Def -> 00uv"},{"DocComment":" where again, `ef` is dependent on D.  And, if D==C, then ef==ab."},{"DocComment":" Note as well that since we never look at the high order bits again,"},{"DocComment":" the XORs by C and D are unnecessary.  Rather than:"},{"DocComment":"    Cxyz ^ Cab0 -> 0Dwz"},{"DocComment":" we can do:"},{"DocComment":"    Cxyz ^ 0ab0 -> CDwz"},{"DocComment":" then:"},{"DocComment":"    CDwz ^ 00ef -> CDuv"},{"DocComment":" as we're just going to return the lowest 16 bits to the caller."},{"DocComment":" Given that we're doing this byte-by-byte and there's only 256 total"},{"DocComment":" potential bytes, we precompute all XORs into the REDUCE_BYTES"},{"DocComment":" buffer.  In the above example:"},{"DocComment":"   REDUCE_BYTES[C] -> ab"},{"DocComment":"   REDUCE_BYTES[D] -> ef"},{"DocComment":" Since we're mapping every byte to a u16, we take up 512B of space"},{"DocComment":" to do this, and our reduction is just a couple of pipelined shifts/XORs."}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":true,"opacity":"Transparent","lang_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"sig