Comment":" The method does no guarding against overflows, so enumerating more than"},{"DocComment":" [`usize::MAX`] elements either produces the wrong result or panics. If"},{"DocComment":" overflow checks are enabled, a panic is guaranteed."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Panics"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The returned iterator might panic if the to-be-returned index would"},{"DocComment":" overflow a [`usize`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`zip`]: Iterator::zip"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let a = ['a', 'b', 'c'];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let mut iter = a.into_iter().enumerate();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some((0, 'a')));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some((1, 'b')));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some((2, 'c')));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), None);"},{"DocComment":" ```"}],"inline":"Hint","rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"enumerate_method"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"A"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[{"clause_id":0,"span":{"data":{"file_id":66,"beg":{"line":980,"col":8},"end":{"line":980,"col":12}},"generated_from_span":null},"origin":"WhereClauseOnImpl","trait_":{"regions":[],"skip_binder":{"id":22,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40277}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}}],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"signature":{"is_unsafe":false,"abi":"Rust","inputs":[{"Deduplicated":40333}],"output":{"HashConsedValue":[41645,{"Adt":{"id":{"Adt":166},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40333}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}]}},"src":{"TraitImpl":{"impl_ref":{"id":140,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40277}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[{"Deduplicated":40334}]}},"trait_ref":{"id":21,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40333},{"Deduplicated":40277}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}},"item_id":{"Method":16},"reuses_default":true}},"is_global_initializer":null,"body":"Opaque"},{"def_id":1634,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["iter",0]},{"Ident":["range",0]},{"Impl":{"Trait":140}},{"Ident":["collect",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":66,"beg":{"line":980,"col":0},"end":{"line":980,"col":40}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Transforms an iterator into a collection."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `collect()` takes ownership of an iterator and produces whichever"},{"DocComment":" collection type you request. The iterator itself carries no knowledge of"},{"DocComment":" the eventual container; the target collection is chosen entirely by the"},{"DocComment":" type you ask `collect()` to return. This makes `collect()` one of the"},{"DocComment":" more powerful methods in the standard library, and it shows up in a wide"},{"DocComment":" variety of contexts."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The most basic pattern in which `collect()` is used is to turn one"},{"DocComment":" collection into another. You take a collection, call [`iter`] on it,"},{"DocComment":" do a bunch of transformations, and then `collect()` at the end."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `collect()` can also create instances of types that are not typical"},{"DocComment":" collections. For example, a [`String`] can be built from [`char`]s,"},{"DocComment":" and an iterator of [`Result<T, E>`][`Result`] items can be collected"},{"DocComment":" into `Result<Collection<T>, E>`. See the examples below for more."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Because `collect()` is so general, it can cause problems with type"},{"DocComment":" inference. As such, `collect()` is one of the few times you'll see"},{"DocComment":" the syntax affectionately known as the 'turbofish': `::<>`. This"},{"DocComment":" helps the inference algorithm understand specifically which collection"},{"DocComment":" you're trying to collect into."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Basic usage:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let a = [1, 2, 3];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let doubled: Vec<i32> = a.iter()"},{"DocComment":"                          .map(|x| x * 2)"},{"DocComment":"                          .collect();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(vec![2, 4, 6], doubled);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Note that we needed the `: Vec<i32>` on the left-hand side. This is because"},{"DocComment":" we could collect into, for example, a [`VecDeque<T>`] instead:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`VecDeque<T>`]: ../../std/collections/struct.VecDeque.html"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::collections::VecDeque;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let a = [1, 2, 3];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let doubled: VecDeque<i32> = a.iter().map(|x| x * 2).collect();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(2, doubled[0]);"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(4, doubled[1]);"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(6, doubled[2]);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Using the 'turbofish' instead of annotating `doubled`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let a = [1, 2, 3];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let doubled = a.iter().map(|x| x * 2).collect::<Vec<i32>>();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(vec![2, 4, 6], doubled);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Because `collect()` only cares about what you're collecting into, you can"},{"DocComment":" still use a partial type hint, `_`, with the turbofish:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let a = [1, 2, 3];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let doubled = a.iter().map(|x| x * 2).collect::<Vec<_>>();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(vec![2, 4, 6], doubled);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Using `collect()` to make a [`String`]:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let chars = ['g', 'd', 'k', 'k', 'n'];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let hello: String = chars.into_iter()"},{"DocComment":"     .map(|x| x as u8)"},{"DocComment":"     .map(|x| (x + 1) as char)"},{"DocComment":"     .collect();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(\"hello\", hello);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If you have a list of [`Result<T, E>`][`Result`]s, you can use `collect()` to"},{"DocComment":" see if any of them failed:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let results = [Ok(1), Err(\"nope\"), Ok(3), Err(\"bad\")];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let result: Result<Vec<_>, &str> = results.into_iter().collect();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // gives us the first error"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(Err(\"nope\"), result);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let results = [Ok(1), Ok(3)];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let result: Result<Vec<_>, &str> = results.into_iter().collect();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // gives us the list of answers"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(Ok(vec![1, 3]), result);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`iter`]: Iterator::next"},{"DocComment":" [`String`]: ../../std/string/struct.String.html"},{"DocComment":" [`char`]: type@char"}],"inline":"Hint","rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"iterator_collect_fn"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"A"},{"index":1,"name":"B"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[{"clause_id":0,"span":{"data":{"file_id":66,"beg":{"line":980,"col":8},"end":{"line":980,"col":12}},"generated_from_span":null},"origin":"WhereClauseOnImpl","trait_":{"regions":[],"skip_binder":{"id":22,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40277}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}},{"clause_id":1,"span":{"data":{"file_id":64,"beg":{"line":2077,"col":18},"end":{"line":2077,"col":42}},"generated_from_span":null},"origin":"WhereClauseOnFn","trait_":{"regions":[],"skip_binder":{"id":36,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40278},{"Deduplicated":40277}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}}],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"signature":{"is_unsafe":false,"abi":"Rust","inputs":[{"Deduplicated":40333}],"output":{"Deduplicated":40278}},"src":{"TraitImpl":{"impl_ref":{"id":140,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40277}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[{"Deduplicated":40334}]}},"trait_ref":{"id":21,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40333},{"Deduplicated":40277}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}},"item_id":{"Method":30},"reuses_default":true}},"is_global_initializer":null,"body":"Opaque"},{"def_id":1635,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["iter",0]},{"Ident":["adapters",0]},{"Ident":["step_by",0]},{"Impl":{"Trait":143}},{"Ident":["step_by",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":60,"beg":{"line":51,"col":0},"end":{"line":53,"col":16}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Creates an iterator starting at the same point, but stepping by"},{"DocComment":" the given amount at each iteration."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Note 1: The first element of the iterator will always be returned,"},{"DocComment":" regardless of the step given."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Note 2: The time at which ignored elements are pulled is not fixed."},{"DocComment":" `StepBy` behaves like the sequence `self.next()`, `self.nth(step-1)`,"},{"DocComment":" `self.nth(step-1)`, …, but is also free to behave like the sequence"},{"DocComment":" `advance_n_and_return_first(&mut self, step)`,"},{"DocComment":" `advance_n_and_return_first(&mut self, step)`, …"},{"DocComment":" Which way is used may change for some iterators for performance reasons."},{"DocComment":" The second way will advance the iterator earlier and may consume more items."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `advance_n_and_return_first` is the equivalent of:"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" fn advance_n_and_return_first<I>(iter: &mut I, n: usize) -> Option<I::Item>"},{"DocComment":" where"},{"DocComment":"     I: Iterator,"},{"DocComment":" {"},{"DocComment":"     let next = iter.next();"},{"DocComment":"     if n > 1 {"},{"DocComment":"         iter.nth(n - 2);"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":"     next"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Panics"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The method will panic if the given step is `0`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let a = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5];"},{"DocComment":" let mut iter = a.into_iter().step_by(2);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(0));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(2));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(4));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), None);"},{"DocComment":" ```"}],"inline":"Hint","rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"I"},{"index":1,"name":"Clause0_Item"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[{"clause_id":0,"span":{"data":{"file_id":60,"beg":{"line":53,"col":7},"end":{"line":53,"col":15}},"generated_from_span":null},"origin":"WhereClauseOnImpl","trait_":{"regions":[],"skip_binder":{"id":21,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40277},{"Deduplicated":40278}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}}],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"signature":{"is_unsafe":false,"abi":"Rust","inputs":[{"Deduplicated":40339},{"Deduplicated":2971}],"output":{"HashConsedValue":[41646,{"Adt":{"id":{"Adt":80},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40339}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}]}},"src":{"TraitImpl":{"impl_ref":{"id":143,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40277},{"Deduplicated":40278}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[{"Deduplicated":40338}]}},"trait_ref":{"id":21,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40339},{"Deduplicated":40278}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}},"item_id":{"Method":7},"reuses_default":true}},"is_global_initializer":null,"body":"Opaque"},{"def_id":1636,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["iter",0]},{"Ident":["adapters",0]},{"Ident":["step_by",0]},{"Impl":{"Trait":143}},{"Ident":["map",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":60,"beg":{"line":51,"col":0},"end":{"line":53,"col":16}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Takes a closure and creates an iterator which calls that closure on each"},{"DocComment":" element."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `map()` transforms one iterator into another, by means of its argument:"},{"DocComment":" something that implements [`FnMut`]. It produces a new iterator which"},{"DocComment":" calls this closure on each element of the original iterator."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If you are good at thinking in types, you can think of `map()` like this:"},{"DocComment":" If you have an iterator that gives you elements of some type `A`, and"},{"DocComment":" you want an iterator of some other type `B`, you can use `map()`,"},{"DocComment":" passing a closure that takes an `A` and returns a `B`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `map()` is conceptually similar to a [`for`] loop. However, as `map()` is"},{"DocComment":" lazy, it is best used when you're already working with other iterators."},{"DocComment":" If you're doing some sort of looping for a side effect, it's considered"},{"DocComment":" more idiomatic to use [`for`] than `map()`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`for`]: ../../book/ch03-05-control-flow.html#looping-through-a-collection-with-for"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Basic usage:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let a = [1, 2, 3];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let mut iter = a.iter().map(|x| 2 * x);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(2));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(4));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(6));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next(), None);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If you're doing some sort of side effect, prefer [`for`] to `map()`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(unused_must_use)]"},{"DocComment":" // don't do this:"},{"DocComment":" (0..5).map(|x| println!(\"{x}\"));"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // it won't even execute, as it is lazy. Rust will warn you about this."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Instead, use a for-loop:"},{"DocComment":" for x in 0..5 {"},{"DocComment":"     println!(\"{x}\");"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"}],"inline":"Hint","rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"IteratorMap"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"I"},{"index":1,"name":"B"},{"index":2,"name":"F"},{"index":3,"name":"Clause0_Item"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[{"clause_id":0,"span":{"data":{"file_id":60,"beg":{"line":53,"col":7},"end":{"line":53,"col":15}},"generated_from_span":null},"origin":"WhereClauseOnImpl","trait_":{"regions":[],"skip_binder":{"id":21,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40277},{"Deduplicated":40317}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}},{"clause_id":1,"span":{"data":{"file_id":64,"beg":{"line":834,"col":11},"end":{"line":834,"col":33}},"generated_from_span":null},"origin":"WhereClauseOnFn","trait_":{"regions":[],"skip_binder":{"id":35,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":40311},{"Deduplicated":40388},{"De